questions.criteria:
1 mark for each valid reason, up to 2:
- their population had suddenly/dramatically declined (by a staggering 86%);
- most people had never heard of them / no one was taking steps to protect them, so raising the status could draw attention to their preservation.

1 mark for each method, up to 2:
- genetic analysis (showing they diverged from a common ancestor / are as genetically distinct as lions and tigers);
- observation (they can be observed living separate existences / their different physical features can be observed).

1 mark for each clear point of difference, up to 3:
- the forest elephant’s forequarters are lower than its hindquarters, giving a more compact shape;
- the forest elephant is smaller and has dark colouring, while the savannah elephant is larger and lighter-coloured;
- the forest elephant has smaller, more rounded ears;
- the savannah elephant has thicker, more curved tusks.
Answers must compare/contrast the two types, not just describe one.

1 mark for each developed point in the student’s own words, up to 3:
- fruit-tree seeds would no longer be spread around, so fruit trees would become less varied/plentiful;
- small trees would no longer be eaten/thinned out, so large trees would have less space to grow;
- with fewer large trees there would be less carbon absorption (worsening climate change) and less support for primates and other animals.
Do not credit answers merely copied from the text.
